UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 37, Lesson 2: Sorting and analysing information', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort into fact or opinion: the library opened in 1962; the old library was friendlier; the council paid for a new roof.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFacts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTreating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSort your notes into three categories. Mark each fact or opinion, write one pattern and one gap, and compare two sources: where do they agree and differ?\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Sort your notes into three categories. Mark each fact or opinion, write one pattern and one gap, and compare two sources: where do they agree and differ? Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services were added after 2000. Fact: \"The library opened a children''s section in 1998.\" Opinion: \"The old library was nicer.\"\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry. Correction: Facts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ.","Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Skip respectful protocols because the first idea looked neat.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new inquiry\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Facts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Facts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.","Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Sort into fact or opinion: the library opened in 1962; the old library was frien\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"List the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Facts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["Facts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.","Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with e\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence. Useful feedback uses the original sources and method.","options":["The new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Inquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant evidence with accurate reasoning and named sources or places.","question":"Inquire into or describe a fresh example that uses the central skill. Show evidence and name the sources or places that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: Example: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services were added after 2000. Fact: \"The library opened a children''s section in 1998.\" Opinion: \"The old library was nicer.\"","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change the claim or evidence, not just make the notes longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected claim and an evidence-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely misconception for this HSIE topic, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry. Correction: Facts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w37-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise sort.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Historical and Geographical Inquiry","success_criteria":["I can use sort with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify pattern and explain why it matters.","I can practise fact independently on a new prompt.","I can notice opinion needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services were added after 2000. Fact: \"The library opened a children''s section in 1998.\" Opinion: \"The old library was nicer.\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort into fact or opinion: the library opened in 1962; the old library was friendlier; the council paid for a new roof.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFacts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.","explain":"Sorting information into categories helps find patterns and gaps. Facts can be checked in sources, while opinions are what people think or feel. Comparing sources shows where they agree and where they differ.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Treating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fact and opinion.","caption":"Facts can be traced to sources.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Fact</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">opened in 1962</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Opinion</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">the old one was friendlier</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services were added after 2000. Fact: \"The library opened a children''s section in 1998.\" Opinion: \"The old library was nicer.\" Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Patterns and gaps.","caption":"Gaps show what to ask next.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Pattern</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">more services after 2000</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Gap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no dates for the roof</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTreating a strong opinion as proven fact weakens the conclusion of an inquiry. Here is a more productive model: Example: for the library inquiry, notes are sorted into buildings, services and people. A pattern appears: more services were added after 2000. Fact: \"The library opened a children''s section in 1998.\" Opinion: \"The old library was nicer.\"\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFacts: opened in 1962; council paid for a new roof. Opinion: the old library was friendlier.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name it